insert into public.questions values
('Q', $qt$INPUT N, TOTAL = 0, FOR I = 1 TO 3, TOTAL = TOTAL + I, NEXT I, OUTPUT TOTAL. What is output?$qt$, $opts$["3","6","5","0"]$opts$::jsonb, 1, $ex$Trace the loop: I = 1 gives TOTAL = 1, I = 2 gives TOTAL = 3, I = 3 gives TOTAL = 6. Output is 6. Stopping the loop early gives 3; adding the counter instead of accumulating gives 5.$ex$, 'check', 001, (OBJ), null),
('Q', $qt$In a flowchart, which shape represents a decision?$qt$, $opts$["Parallelogram","Rectangle","Rounded rectangle","Diamond"]$opts$::jsonb, 3, $ex$The diamond tests a condition and has two exits (yes/no). The parallelogram is input/output, the rectangle is a process, and the rounded rectangle starts or stops the chart.$ex$, 'check', 002, (OBJ), null),
('Q', $qt$X = 0, WHILE X < 3 DO X = X + 1, OUTPUT X, ENDWHILE. What is the full output?$qt$, $opts$["1 2 3","0 1 2 3","1 2","3 2 1"]$opts$::jsonb, 0, $ex$Three passes give outputs 1, 2, then 3, and the fourth test (3 < 3) is false so the loop stops. Including the starting 0 confuses the initial value with an output; stopping a pass early drops the 3.$ex$, 'check', 101, (OBJ), null),
('Q', $qt$X = 1, WHILE X < 5 DO OUTPUT X, ENDWHILE. The loop never ends. Which change fixes it?$qt$, $opts$["Change OUTPUT X to OUTPUT X + 1","Change X = 1 to X = 0","Add X = X + 1 before ENDWHILE","Change < to > in the condition"]$opts$::jsonb, 2, $ex$X never changes, so X < 5 stays true forever; incrementing X inside the loop lets it reach 5 and exit. Changing the OUTPUT line leaves X untouched; starting at 0 is still infinite; flipping the test to 1 > 5 ends the loop by producing nothing at all.$ex$, 'check', 102, (OBJ), null),
('Q', $qt$A program accepts marks from 0 to 100. Which value is boundary test data?$qt$, $opts$["56","-3","100","eighty"]$opts$::jsonb, 2, $ex$100 sits exactly on the allowed edge, so it tests where the limit lies. 56 is normal, -3 is extreme (valid kind, far outside), and eighty is abnormal (wrong data type).$ex$, 'check', 201, (OBJ), null),
('Q', $qt$Which validation check rejects the value 12A entered as an age?$qt$, $opts$["Type check","Range check","Presence check","Length check"]$opts$::jsonb, 0, $ex$Age must be numeric and 12A is not, so the type check rejects it. A range check tests value bounds, a presence check tests for empty input, and a length check counts characters.$ex$, 'check', 202, (OBJ), null),
('Q', $qt$Which of these describes double entry verification?$qt$, $opts$["Reading data back from disk after saving","Checking the data type as it is entered","Entering the data once, very carefully","Typing the data twice and comparing the copies"]$opts$::jsonb, 3, $ex$Double entry means two independent entries that the computer compares, catching slips by either typist. Reading back from disk checks storage, a type check is validation, and careful single entry is just hope.$ex$, 'check', 301, (OBJ), null),
('Q', $qt$A student copies numbers from paper to computer, then reads the screen back against the paper. This is an example of what?$qt$, $opts$["Validation","Visual check","Range check","Boundary test"]$opts$::jsonb, 1, $ex$Comparing entered data against its source is verification by visual check. Validation would judge whether the numbers are sensible; range and boundary belong to validation, not verification.$ex$, 'check', 302, (OBJ), null),
('Q', $qt$Trace this algorithm with A = 4: INPUT A, B = 0, FOR I = 1 TO A, B = B + I, NEXT I, OUTPUT B. What is output?$qt$, $opts$["7","10","14","4"]$opts$::jsonb, 1, $ex$Marks as awarded: I running 1 to 4 with B accumulating 1, 3, 6, 10 [1]; B = 10 [1]. Answer 10. Stopping at I = 3 gives 6 and loses the method mark; adding I itself instead of accumulating gives 4.$ex$, 'exam', 900, (OBJ), (STMT '7')),
('Q', $qt$INPUT N, IF N MOD 2 = 0 THEN OUTPUT N / 2 ELSE OUTPUT N * 3 + 1, ENDIF, with N = 6. What is output?$qt$, $opts$["13","9","3","19"]$opts$::jsonb, 2, $ex$Marks as awarded: 6 MOD 2 = 0, so the IF branch runs [1]; 6 / 2 = 3 [1]. Answer 3. Taking the ELSE branch gives 19 and scores 0; halving without testing gives 3 for the wrong reason and loses the method mark.$ex$, 'exam', 901, (OBJ), (STMT '7'));